  category: "Monitoring & Observability / Security Operations",
  description: "SIEM and observability solve different problems, but the line between them is disappearing fast. Here's a plain English breakdown of what each one does, where they overlap, and why the smartest teams in 2026 aren't picking one over the other.",
  slug: "siem-vs-observability-differences-overlap-converging",
};

const FEATURED_GUIDES = [
  { title: "What Is AI Observability? (2026 Guide)", link: "/blog/what-is-ai-observability-complete-guide" },
  { title: "AI Agent Observability: How to Monitor", link: "/blog/ai-agent-observability-how-to-monitor" },
  { title: "SIEM vs Observability: Differences & Convergence", link: "/blog/siem-vs-observability-differences-overlap-converging" },
  { title: "Observability vs Monitoring: Key Differences", link: "/blog/observability-vs-monitoring-key-differences" },
  { title: "Website Uptime Monitoring Complete Guide", link: "/blog/website-uptime-monitoring-complete-guide" },
  { title: "Best Uptime Monitoring Tools in 2026", link: "/blog/best-uptime-monitoring-tools-2026" },
];

const PLATFORM_SPECIALS = [
  { title: "August 2026 Release Notes", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "June 2026 Release Notes", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "May 2026 (Phase 2 — Agent API)", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "AI Agent API & Open API", link: "/docs/api-for-agents" },
  { title: "MCP Server Setup Guide", link: "/docs/mcp" },
  { title: "Self-Host Air-Gapped Setup", link: "/self-host" },
];

const FAQS = [
  {
    "q": "Is a SIEM the same thing as observability?",
    "a": "No, but they're closer than most people assume. A SIEM is purpose built around detecting malicious activity using security specific correlation and threat intelligence. Observability is broader, focused on understanding any system behavior, operational or otherwise, through logs, metrics, and traces. They run on overlapping data and, in a converged platform, feed the same investigative pipeline."
  },
  {
    "q": "Do I need both, or can I get away with just one?",
    "a": "If you have any production system handling real user data, you need both capabilities in some form, even if they're not both dedicated standalone products. Security without operational context misses incidents that look operational until you dig in. Operations without security context misses incidents that look like a bug until you realize someone caused it on purpose."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why do SIEM alerts pile up so badly?",
    "a": "Because detection and investigation have traditionally been treated as two separate jobs. The SIEM's job is to detect, and a human's job is to figure out if the detection matters. There are never enough humans relative to the volume a well-tuned detection engine produces, so alerts queue faster than anyone can work them, and turning down sensitivity becomes the path of least resistance, which is exactly how real threats slip through."
  },
  {
    "q": "What's the actual difference between a security incident and an operational incident, in practice?",
    "a": "Often, nothing, until you investigate. A latency spike, an unusual traffic pattern, or an elevated error rate can be caused by either a bug or an attacker, and the surface level signal frequently looks identical. The distinguishing evidence, such as who's involved, what changed, and whether the source matches known bad patterns, usually only emerges once someone, or something, investigates, which is why treating detection and investigation as one motion matters so much."
  },
  {
    "q": "Does observability data help with security investigations?",
    "a": "Enormously, and this is underused by most teams. Deploy history, service ownership, request traces, and infrastructure topology, all classic observability context, are exactly what a security analyst needs to understand the blast radius of a suspicious event and distinguish a real attack from a false positive."
  },
  {
    "q": "How does AI change this equation?",
    "a": "Two ways. First, AI agents running in production create an entirely new category of risk, prompt injection, tool abuse, and runaway cost, that doesn't map cleanly onto either traditional SIEM rules or traditional performance metrics, so it needs dedicated detection either way. Second, AI driven investigation is what solves the alert fatigue and manual correlation problems that have plagued both disciplines for years, by automating the evidence gathering and correlation work a human analyst used to have to do by hand, on every single incident, all day."
  },
  {
    "q": "What does a converged SIEM and observability platform look like day to day?",
    "a": "Fewer tools open during an incident, for one thing. Instead of a SIEM console, a metrics dashboard, a log search tool, and a threat intel lookup, all telemetry lives in one normalized pipeline. Detections, whether they're security rules or operational thresholds, open the same kind of first-class incident, and an AI analyst investigates it automatically, returning a verdict backed by evidence rather than leaving a human to reconstruct the timeline from scratch."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is this convergence just a repackaging trend, or a real technical shift?",
    "a": "It's real, and it's driven by something more fundamental than marketing. The data underlying both disciplines has always overlapped heavily, and the cost of keeping it artificially separated, duplicated ingestion, slower incident response, and analysts burning out on incomplete pictures, has become large enough that fixing it is worth the architectural effort. The trend isn't calling two things by one name. It's building one pipeline that answers both kinds of questions."
  }
];
